================================================================================
PatchClosure-Bench case: dos_rack_byte_ranges  (REBUILT 2026-07-15, real-app-http)
Rack::Files HTTP byte-range / multipart-assembly DoS (CWE-400 / CWE-770, dos-threshold)
CVE-2024-26141 (V1=rack 3.1.20, INCOMPLETE) -> CVE-2026-34826 (V2=rack 3.1.21, COMPLETE)
================================================================================

HARD-POLICY COMPLIANCE
  Previous build triggered Rack::Files IN-PROCESS (APP.call(build_env(...)) inside the container). BANNED.
  This build serves the REAL published Rack::Files from a REAL running Rack web app (WEBrick via the
  published rackup handler) and triggers the DoS with an EXTERNAL HTTP GET /file carrying a crafted
  Range header, measured as SERVER-SIDE response latency (external client round-trip).
    run_mode="real-app-http", runs_real_software=true, external_http_trigger=true, is_verbatim_lift=false.

VERSIONS PINNED (RubyGems, ruby:3.2-slim, gem install rack -v <ver> + webrick + rackup)
  V0 = rack 3.0.9   (pre CVE-2024-26141; no aggregate check)              port 28241
  V1 = rack 3.1.20  (CVE-2024-26141 fix = TARGET; total-coverage only)    port 28243   <-- incomplete
  V2 = rack 3.1.21  (CVE-2026-34826 fix; adds max_ranges:100 count cap)   port 28244

Live get_byte_ranges signature read back over HTTP (GET /sig) inside each container:
  V0: req:http_range,req:size
  V1: req:http_range,req:size
  V2: req:http_range,req:size,key:max_ranges     <-- only V2 has max_ranges

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL WEB APP (app/server.rb)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WEBrick (Rackup::Handler::WEBrick) serves a generated 900000-byte file /data/file through the REAL
  published Rack::Files middleware:  files = Rack::Files.new('/data');  GET /file -> files.call(env).
  Rack::Files honours env['HTTP_RANGE'] via Rack::Utils.get_byte_ranges -> the DoS sink. Endpoints:
  GET /health (readiness), GET /file (the sink), GET /sig (reflection: live get_byte_ranges signature).
  Server config: WEBrick's 112 KiB header cap (WEBrick::HTTPRequest::MAX_HEADER_LENGTH) is raised to
  8 MiB so the ~2.8 MB residual Range header is admitted (analogous to nginx large_client_header_buffers)
  -- a WEB-SERVER config change, NOT a change to Rack. Rack is installed unmodified from RubyGems.
  (Finding: default HTTP-server header caps ~80-114 KiB incidentally blunt the count-DoS unless the
   front-end is configured for large headers; Puma caps a single header value at 80 KiB, WEBrick 112 KiB.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SINK: Rack::Utils.get_byte_ranges (lib/rack/utils.rb) evolution (verbatim, real gem tags)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
V0  rack 3.0.9  -- NO total-coverage check, NO count cap:
    def get_byte_ranges(http_range, size)
      return nil unless http_range && http_range =~ /bytes=([^;]+)/
      ranges = []
      $1.split(/,\s*/).each { |spec| ...parse/clamp...; ranges << (r0..r1) if r0 <= r1 }
      ranges                       # <-- returned unbounded; no aggregate check at all
    end

V1  rack 3.1.20  -- CVE-2024-26141: TOTAL-COVERAGE check ADDED (count still unbounded):
    def get_byte_ranges(http_range, size)         # signature UNCHANGED vs V0
      ... same parsing loop ...
      return [] if ranges.map(&:size).sum > size   # <-- reject if summed range size exceeds file
      ranges
    end

V2  rack 3.1.21  -- CVE-2026-34826: range-COUNT cap ADDED (total-coverage check retained):
    def get_byte_ranges(http_range, size, max_ranges: 100)   # <-- new keyword
      return nil unless http_range && http_range =~ /bytes=([^;]+)/
      byte_range = $1
      return nil if byte_range.count(',') >= max_ranges       # <-- CVE-2026-34826 count cap
      ranges = []; byte_range.split(/,\s*/).each { |spec| ... }
      return [] if ranges.map(&:size).sum > size              # CVE-2024-26141 guard retained
      ranges
    end
  (commit 345a4cfa51f451e58b2931322998e04f3cf6dc0d, "Use a default limit of 100 byte ranges.")

Rack::Files consumes this: nil -> whole file (200); [] -> 416; multi-range -> 206 multipart/byteranges
(BaseIterator#each: one multipart heading + a seek/read per range). Cost ~ O(#ranges + total bytes).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ATTACK: EXTERNAL HTTP  GET /file  with a crafted Range header (file size = 900000 bytes)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
original (CVE-2024-26141):  Range: bytes=0-899999,0-899999,...  (6000 full-file ranges, header 54 KB)
    total byte coverage = 6000 * 900000 = 5.4 GB >> 900000
    => V0 assembles multi-GB and OOM-crashes the 512m container (memory DoS);
       V1 guard `ranges.map(&:size).sum > size` fires -> [] -> 416.

residual (CVE-2026-34826): Range: bytes=0-0,0-0,0-0,...         (700000 x 1-byte range, header 2.8 MB)
    summed size = 700000 * 1 = 700000 <= 900000  -> PASSES V1's total-coverage check,
    but 700000 ranges force 700000 multipart parts (~52 MB body). V1 fix INCOMPLETE.

benign:                     Range: bytes=0-99                    (single normal 100-byte range)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MEASURED SERVER-SIDE LATENCY (real Docker; ruby:3.2-slim; WEBrick+rackup; cpus=1.0, mem_limit=512m;
FRESH server brought up per probe -> a crash never poisons the next; 15s client read timeout;
body streamed + discarded client-side)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                 V0 (3.0.9)                    V1 (3.1.20, TARGET)   V2 (3.1.21)
  original      CRASH/OOM  [reset ~1150ms]      13.2 ms  [416]        4.7 ms   [200]
                (-> 15000ms sentinel)
  residual      2597.6 ms  [206]                2860.5 ms [206]       141.3 ms [200]
  benign           1.0 ms  [206]                 1.6 ms  [206]          3.9 ms [206]

  HTTP status in [] : 206 = multipart/partial assembled (costly path); 416 = range rejected
                      (total-coverage guard); 200 = whole file served (count cap -> nil).
  V0 original is OOMKilled=true (docker inspect .State.OOMKilled) -> DoS-positive.

  Thresholds: T_FAST = 50 ms, T_SLOW = 1000 ms, crash/timeout sentinel = 15000 ms.
  V1 residual DoS amplification over benign   = 2860.5 / 1.6   = 1787.8x
  V1 -> V2 residual reduction (fix closes it) = 2860.5 / 141.3 = 20.2x

Standalone PoC scripts (verified against a fresh V1, http://127.0.0.1:28243):
  original_poc.py  -> {"status":416,"latency_ms":19.3,"crash":false}         (BLOCKED on V1)
  bypass_poc.py    -> {"status":206,"latency_ms":2578.8,"bytes":51800014}    (REACHES/slow on V1)

